1
00:00:02,276 --> 00:00:04,168
So now that we know these 2 approaches,

2
00:00:04,521 --> 00:00:08,529
adjacency matrices and adjacency lists, the

3
00:00:08,593 --> 00:00:11,013
obvious question is which one is better?

4
00:00:11,318 --> 00:00:12,264
Which one should you use?

5
00:00:12,937 --> 00:00:14,573
And for that we're actually going to compare

6
00:00:14,781 --> 00:00:15,951
the time complexity

7
00:00:16,528 --> 00:00:18,949
for various operations and the space

8
00:00:18,949 --> 00:00:19,639
complexity

9
00:00:20,216 --> 00:00:23,118
of those 2 approaches. We'll have a look at

10
00:00:23,118 --> 00:00:24,897
the insert time complexity,

11
00:00:25,506 --> 00:00:28,055
the time complexity for finding a possible

12
00:00:28,088 --> 00:00:29,498
connection between 2 nodes,

13
00:00:30,092 --> 00:00:32,291
and the time complexity for finding all

14
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:34,555
adjacent nodes of a given node.

15
00:00:35,615 --> 00:00:37,365
Now when it comes to inserting with an

16
00:00:37,397 --> 00:00:40,561
adjacency matrix, the time complexity is O,

17
00:00:41,412 --> 00:00:43,354
linear time. Now why is that?

18
00:00:44,141 --> 00:00:45,522
If we have a matrix like this

19
00:00:46,148 --> 00:00:47,401
and we insert a new node,

20
00:00:48,011 --> 00:00:49,584
not in the graph on the left but in the

21
00:00:49,584 --> 00:00:51,559
matrix, let's say that new node is there,

22
00:00:52,089 --> 00:00:54,835
a node with value 4, with an identifier of

23
00:00:54,835 --> 00:00:55,172
4,

24
00:00:55,947 --> 00:00:58,532
then this is how our matrix grows. So we

25
00:00:58,532 --> 00:01:00,727
have to add a lot of new elements to the

26
00:01:00,727 --> 00:01:01,198
matrix.

27
00:01:02,333 --> 00:01:04,569
So in the end, we have to add a new row

28
00:01:05,534 --> 00:01:08,012
and add an entry for connections to every

29
00:01:08,125 --> 00:01:10,699
other node in our graph, and we have to

30
00:01:10,699 --> 00:01:13,635
revisit all existing entries and represent

31
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:15,750
the connection to our new node there as

32
00:01:15,928 --> 00:01:16,057
well.

33
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:21,093
So depending on how you do this, you could

34
00:01:21,206 --> 00:01:22,933
even end up with quadratic time,

35
00:01:23,492 --> 00:01:25,082
But you can also come up with

36
00:01:25,114 --> 00:01:27,956
implementations that give you linear time on

37
00:01:28,133 --> 00:01:28,438
average.

38
00:01:29,755 --> 00:01:31,907
So no matter what though, no matter if it's

39
00:01:31,907 --> 00:01:34,654
linear or even worse quadratic time, you're

40
00:01:34,654 --> 00:01:37,031
not going to have a great time complexity

41
00:01:37,063 --> 00:01:37,175
there.

42
00:01:38,348 --> 00:01:42,555
Now for an adjacency list, this actually can

43
00:01:42,604 --> 00:01:45,237
be achieved in a way more efficient way.

44
00:01:45,655 --> 00:01:48,433
Depending on how you implement it, you can

45
00:01:48,466 --> 00:01:51,196
get constant time inserting values.

46
00:01:51,742 --> 00:01:54,054
And well, it should be obvious that this is

47
00:01:54,166 --> 00:01:57,169
better than O or even O.

48
00:01:58,485 --> 00:02:00,813
Now why that is the case is something we'll

49
00:02:00,813 --> 00:02:01,873
explore later.

50
00:02:02,917 --> 00:02:05,855
Now for finding edges between nodes in an

51
00:02:05,952 --> 00:02:07,927
adjacency matrix, that's O.

52
00:02:08,858 --> 00:02:11,363
Because as I showed before, if you have your

53
00:02:11,427 --> 00:02:14,462
adjacency matrix, you can get the connection

54
00:02:14,478 --> 00:02:18,023
between 2 nodes simply by accessing the

55
00:02:18,103 --> 00:02:20,815
appropriate identifiers, the indexes of

56
00:02:20,847 --> 00:02:23,429
those nodes, and you get a result. You get

57
00:02:23,429 --> 00:02:25,265
an instant result whether a connection

58
00:02:25,298 --> 00:02:27,194
between those nodes exists or not.

59
00:02:28,830 --> 00:02:31,131
And therefore accessing by index of course

60
00:02:31,147 --> 00:02:33,772
is O, so we have a great time complexity

61
00:02:33,870 --> 00:02:33,999
here.

62
00:02:34,990 --> 00:02:39,209
For adjacency lists, that often is O, though

63
00:02:39,370 --> 00:02:41,432
depending on the implementation, we can

64
00:02:41,609 --> 00:02:43,993
actually achieve O there as well.

65
00:02:44,837 --> 00:02:47,454
And again, that is something you will see

66
00:02:47,663 --> 00:02:49,991
later throughout the next lectures when we

67
00:02:50,055 --> 00:02:52,849
actually build a whole data structure around

68
00:02:53,042 --> 00:02:54,102
an adjacency list.

69
00:02:55,450 --> 00:02:58,694
For finding all adjacent nodes, it's O for

70
00:02:58,742 --> 00:03:01,359
the adjacency matrix because we basically

71
00:03:01,712 --> 00:03:04,747
need to loop through all the entries for a

72
00:03:04,747 --> 00:03:05,293
given node.

73
00:03:06,112 --> 00:03:08,600
For the adjacency list, again, this can be

74
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:09,853
achieved with O.

75
00:03:12,068 --> 00:03:14,926
In case you wonder why it's not O in the

76
00:03:14,943 --> 00:03:17,817
adjacency matrix case, keep in mind that

77
00:03:17,833 --> 00:03:20,305
with an adjacency matrix you have all those

78
00:03:20,434 --> 00:03:23,950
zeros, all those false values, and if you

79
00:03:24,031 --> 00:03:26,375
only wanna find the active connections, you

80
00:03:26,375 --> 00:03:28,623
need to filter those out, which is why you

81
00:03:28,623 --> 00:03:30,421
need to visit all entries

82
00:03:30,999 --> 00:03:33,697
in a given row and then just find the true,

83
00:03:34,210 --> 00:03:35,126
the 1 entries.

84
00:03:36,462 --> 00:03:38,308
So you need to step through all the entries

85
00:03:38,406 --> 00:03:40,333
and check whether they're false or true.

86
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:45,065
For an adjacency list, that's not the case.

87
00:03:45,371 --> 00:03:48,494
If you retrieve the edges for a given node

88
00:03:49,009 --> 00:03:51,681
by identifier, you only get the active

89
00:03:51,729 --> 00:03:53,389
connections. You don't need to visit them

90
00:03:53,502 --> 00:03:56,599
all to filter out the false values. Instead,

91
00:03:56,647 --> 00:03:58,486
you only get the active connections.

92
00:04:00,293 --> 00:04:02,924
That's why we have O for adjacency lists

93
00:04:03,119 --> 00:04:05,457
down there and O for adjacency matrices.

94
00:04:07,237 --> 00:04:10,188
Now we also can think about space complexity

95
00:04:10,204 --> 00:04:12,481
here because there we'll also notice a

96
00:04:12,529 --> 00:04:12,850
difference.

97
00:04:13,507 --> 00:04:15,239
For adjacency matrices,

98
00:04:15,816 --> 00:04:18,350
we'll have a space complexity of O.

99
00:04:19,264 --> 00:04:20,804
Makes a lot of sense because we have a

100
00:04:20,804 --> 00:04:22,086
2-dimensional array

101
00:04:22,792 --> 00:04:24,989
where all slots are filled.

102
00:04:25,534 --> 00:04:28,821
So if we have 4 nodes, we have 16 array

103
00:04:28,902 --> 00:04:29,351
values,

104
00:04:30,024 --> 00:04:31,756
and that is of course a lot of space in

105
00:04:31,788 --> 00:04:33,167
memory which we occupy.

106
00:04:33,776 --> 00:04:36,356
For adjacency lists, it's better. There we

107
00:04:36,356 --> 00:04:37,301
have O

108
00:04:38,102 --> 00:04:38,279
,

109
00:04:38,824 --> 00:04:39,849
and what does this mean?

110
00:04:40,522 --> 00:04:42,910
Well, it means the number of nodes plus the

111
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:43,775
number of edges.

112
00:04:45,233 --> 00:04:47,092
And again, that is something that is easy to

113
00:04:47,092 --> 00:04:49,495
see here. This is our adjacency list,

114
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:53,549
and we see that we have 3 lists, and then in

115
00:04:53,645 --> 00:04:57,010
those lists we have 3 items in total. So we

116
00:04:57,010 --> 00:05:00,199
have 3 nodes plus 3 edges, we have 6 items.

117
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:02,275
That we store in memory here.

118
00:05:04,060 --> 00:05:06,571
For the adjacency matrix, we have 3 nodes,

119
00:05:06,750 --> 00:05:09,035
but since we need to store it as a matrix,

120
00:05:09,099 --> 00:05:11,871
we have 9 items in memory that need to be

121
00:05:11,871 --> 00:05:12,243
stored.

122
00:05:13,680 --> 00:05:16,610
9 values that we need to store to represent

123
00:05:16,610 --> 00:05:17,995
the connections appropriately.

124
00:05:19,026 --> 00:05:21,200
And because of that, it should be easy to

125
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:23,840
see that the adjacency list often is a

126
00:05:23,856 --> 00:05:26,124
better choice, and therefore we'll use this

127
00:05:26,269 --> 00:05:26,993
in this course.

128
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:29,711
And in the next lectures, we'll now build a

129
00:05:29,711 --> 00:05:30,515
complete graph data structure

130
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:32,743
from scratch

131
00:05:33,402 --> 00:05:35,749
where we'll have methods for adding nodes

132
00:05:35,749 --> 00:05:36,649
and for adding edges,

133
00:05:37,501 --> 00:05:39,704
and that data structure will then use an

134
00:05:39,704 --> 00:05:41,505
adjacency list under the hood.
